//BUG="the operands of an operator are evaluated in whatever order the C compiler picks"
//IN="10 3"
//OUT="7"

// `a - b` is lowered to the call `operator-(a, b)`, and codegen
// (`IRExpression::InstanceCall` in src/compiler/codegen/mod.rs) emits that as
// a plain C call with both operands written inline as arguments:
//
//     func12(func7(), func7())
//
// C leaves the order in which arguments are evaluated unspecified. gcc on
// x86-64 evaluates them right to left, so the second `read_i32()` in the
// source runs first, the operands arrive swapped, and this prints -7. clang
// evaluates left to right, so the very same generated C prints 7 there —
// the answer depends on the compiler the judge happens to use, and on its
// version and flags.
//
// Reading two values in one expression is everyday code in this language's
// niche: `read_i32() - read_i32()`, `read_i64() * read_i64()`, a struct
// built from two reads. 50 is the same defect for the arguments of an
// ordinary call.
//
// The language has to pin the order down (left to right, as written), and
// codegen has to enforce it — by evaluating arguments that have side effects
// into temporaries, one statement each, before the call.

fn main
    d = read_i32() - read_i32()
    out "{d}"
